{:edb/db-before-t 1074 :edb/db-after-t 1075 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534387 50 #inst "2026-10-06T19:30:02.226Z" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087489 1100 "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/806/Source/Images/e5a5837a105098f1a551eb7f338017a2.png)" 13194139534387 false] [17592186087489 1100 "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087489 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087489 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$x = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0)$.\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n$x=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$" 13194139534387 false] [17592186087490 1100 "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6)$." 13194139534387 false] [17592186087490 1100 "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087490 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087490 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n$x= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6)$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$x=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$" 13194139534387 false] [17592186087551 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087551 1100 "The quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/806/Source/Images/f94daa181a489d90232901e1c3c781aa.png)\n\n$x = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$?" 13194139534387 false] [17592186087551 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087551 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$x = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6$. So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are\n$x=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/806/Source/Images/a6dac4cb26046fd6ff579751230819f8.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to\n\n$$\n\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right]\n$$\n\nwe get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right]\n$$" 13194139534387 false] [17592186087555 1100 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087555 1100 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$x=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi$." 13194139534387 false] [17592186087555 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta$. We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{x - 2}{3} & = \\cos   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( x - 2 {)}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & = {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( x - 2 {)}^{2}}{9} & = {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  .\\end{array}$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{y + 4}{3} & = \\sin   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 4 {)}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 4 {)}^{2}}{9} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  .\\end{array}$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta & = 1 \\\\ \\frac{( x - 2 {)}^{2}}{9} + \\frac{( y + 4 {)}^{2}}{9} & = 1 \\\\ ( x - 2 {)}^{2} + ( y + 4 {)}^{2} & = 9.\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9\n$$" 13194139534387 false] [17592186087555 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186087679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285395 13194139534387 true] [17592186087679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285402 13194139534387 true] [17592186087679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285459 13194139534387 true] [17592186087679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285483 13194139534387 true] [17592186087679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285490 13194139534387 true] [17592186087680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285409 13194139534387 true] [17592186087680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285437 13194139534387 true] [17592186087680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285465 13194139534387 true] [17592186087680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285497 13194139534387 true] [17592186087680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285504 13194139534387 true] [17592186087725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285416 13194139534387 true] [17592186087725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285423 13194139534387 true] [17592186087725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285470 13194139534387 true] [17592186087725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285511 13194139534387 true] [17592186087727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285430 13194139534387 true] [17592186087727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285444 13194139534387 true] [17592186087727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285451 13194139534387 true] [17592186087727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285476 13194139534387 true] [17592186087727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285518 13194139534387 true] [17592186088306 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534387 true] [17592186088306 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/dd128890a13c0aa79d293b70063eeb8b78e07f56dd42774699d1b2f32e43dd0d.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186088306 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/806/Source/Images/q-78681.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 7\\cos \\theta , y = 7\\sin \\theta $.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta $?" 13194139534387 false] [17592186088306 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=7.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/25503e83f9a196cab4ded2fd93b09028b1608bd7d815edd538564e1aa611ca86.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285395 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285396 13194139534387 true] [17592186285395 1089 "q-105915" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285395 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534387 true] [17592186285395 1100 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $15$ centered at the origin?" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285395 1113 #uuid "384a4738-eb41-5445-8951-5cd80374c2fd" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285395 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of r