 ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=4.$ Substituting $r=4$ gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285461 13194139534387 true] [17592186285460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285462 13194139534387 true] [17592186285460 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534387 true] [17592186285460 1041 "field-1" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285460 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285462 13194139534387 true] [17592186285460 1110 #uuid "7f616619-8511-5b23-8be9-c79aca45f14a" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285461 1034 "3\\cos(\\theta)" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285461 1111 #uuid "daebd9d4-da86-5cf6-a677-5a05309ae2dc" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285462 1034 "4\\cos(\\theta)" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285462 1111 #uuid "e662d6fb-9d1f-5288-b415-ab5ba9404968" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285463 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285464 13194139534387 true] [17592186285463 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534387 true] [17592186285463 1041 "field-2" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285463 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285464 13194139534387 true] [17592186285463 1110 #uuid "c91b0b0f-be2e-58d1-be3e-d427f43b2776" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285464 1034 "4\\sin(\\theta)" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285464 1111 #uuid "41d32f64-ba33-5e3b-b3e3-60fafcc1b15c" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285465 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285466 13194139534387 true] [17592186285465 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285468 13194139534387 true] [17592186285465 1089 "q-321939" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285465 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534387 true] [17592186285465 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/460b94dd8d68c7ed7e5bab45befb9c86496291ae7f192ab022e47493807525d5.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $5$ centered at $(-3,4)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,4).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285465 1113 #uuid "edaa1443-8d9e-5286-baa3-bc89f3671fa3" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285465 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $5$ centered at the point $(-3,4).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285467 13194139534387 true] [17592186285466 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534387 true] [17592186285466 1041 "field-1" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285466 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285467 13194139534387 true] [17592186285466 1110 #uuid "885b9121-05a0-59c5-bce9-25b0594a7877" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285467 1034 "5\\cos(\\theta)-3" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285467 1111 #uuid "5ba2637c-f56a-5aad-9a68-50523954ad26" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285469 13194139534387 true] [17592186285468 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534387 true] [17592186285468 1041 "field-2" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285468 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285469 13194139534387 true] [17592186285468 1110 #uuid "a5bf5171-4f66-54fc-ac7f-1e659a08c462" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285469 1034 "5\\sin(\\theta)+4" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285469 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285469 1111 #uuid "ecf9ae45-8904-55d0-802e-38c3aef371cc" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285470 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285471 13194139534387 true] [17592186285470 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285474 13194139534387 true] [17592186285470 1089 "q-321948" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285470 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534387 true] [17592186285470 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/ed6f528f7e2b873812d492cbeec386ab56af626a964e98b2929824ec9075d320.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285470 1113 #uuid "3f6fa581-0ad2-5257-8314-2e5550da3def" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285470 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285472 13194139534387 true] [17592186285471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285473 13194139534387 true] [17592186285471 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534387 true] [17592186285471 1041 "field-1" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285471 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285473 13194139534387 true] [17592186285471 1110 #uuid "f2fedb1f-671d-57c8-aadc-46276312262d" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285472 1034 "1" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285472 1111 #uuid "dd8ff7a2-0f61-525d-a304-ad1c14eb6d8c" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285473 1034 "0" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285473 1111 #uuid "9601c170-7b2e-5c66-9342-9a6a3b9dfb3e" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285475 13194139534387 true] [17592186285474 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534387 true] [17592186285474 1041 "field-2" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285474 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285475 13194139534387 true] [17592186285474 1110 #uuid "f063c384-0a90-5a58-b263-bace855ed6ae" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285475 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285475 1111 #uuid "601ca41a-42dc-5d5a-9575-3ff7aa048d06" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285476 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285477 13194139534387 true] [17592186285476 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285479 13194139534387 true] [17592186285476 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285481 13194139534387 true] [17592186285476 1089 "q-321989" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285476 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534387 true] [17592186285476 1100 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{11}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139534387 true] [17592186285476 1113 #uuid "03d69679-f524-5057-8f75-9945b7cf6ef2" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285476 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285478 13194139534387 true] [17592186285477 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534387 true] [17592186285477 1041 "field-1" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285477 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285478 13194139534387 true] [17592186285477 1110 #uuid "b98d5869-8856-5222-8f73-96204957f941" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285478 1034 "\\frac{7}{2}" 131941395343