q-79210" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285511 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534387 true] [17592186285511 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/4d98583cd4066bc79695d9ceb75393d80c3a341e1a44c76a2144058da79bb5ca.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285511 1113 #uuid "deee9493-d2a1-55ca-a638-e9ba7b8a8aab" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285511 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-4).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/b71fec9561c150627ca6cfce06e8e79a9fa77f9395930f4bb9cf1a755cf350b2.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285513 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285514 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285515 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285516 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285517 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1041 "selection" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285515 13194139534387 true] [17592186285512 1110 #uuid "9ace54cc-89fa-5885-8314-306137ac2df5" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285513 1034 "[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285513 1111 #uuid "b22a3ccf-95a1-5e42-ae56-251a25a3e126" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285514 1034 "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285514 1111 #uuid "9a1ba595-03f3-5b76-aa2f-d2d15ded6142" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285515 1034 "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285515 1111 #uuid "e2eb507b-780a-5a44-b515-fd429e115ae0" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285516 1034 "[0,\\pi ]" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285516 1111 #uuid "80b00e7b-a374-5784-8dea-d1e04d4c65d0" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285517 1034 "[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285517 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285517 1111 #uuid "febd70cf-e59b-5da6-973f-162d74b42651" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285518 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285519 13194139534387 true] [17592186285518 1089 "q-79221" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285518 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534387 true] [17592186285518 1100 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285518 1113 #uuid "480062d9-9533-5d48-bc7f-73b46822f0d5" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285518 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+3}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285520 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285521 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285522 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285523 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285524 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1041 "selection" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285520 13194139534387 true] [17592186285519 1110 #uuid "e7f557df-3792-5d35-9fc6-05c142f12f3c" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285520 1034 "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534387 true] [17592186285520 1111 #uuid "1fb737d9-6de8-5e97-8000-4ef442ee10f6" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285521 1034 "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9" 13194139534387 true] [17592186285521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 1